# Target C: close seven-no-root `D=-2,k=5,d=0,p=6`

Date: 2026-09-21

Status: exact closure of the last `D=-2,k=5` seven-no-root arithmetic branch. Together with the previously closed `(d,p)=(2,0)` and `(1,3)` cases, there is no seven-no-root counterexample with `D=-2,k=5`.

Companion exact certificate:

`research/verify_target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_seven_noroot_k5_dminus2_d0p6_closure_2026_09_21.py`

It uses the already committed finite-search engine

`research/verify_target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_six_noroot_k4_d2_singleton_capacity_2026_09_21.py`.

## Reduction already established

Suppose a seven-no-root state existed with

`D=-2, k=5, d=0, p=6`.

Its coefficient-constraint space `P <= Q^7` has rank two. The all-depth deletion theorem for defect `-2` implies that every one- and two-coordinate puncture preserves rank two.

For every one-row deletion, defect transport gives a synchronized six-row shadow of defect zero and incomplete-root cost six. The six-row `k=4` containment theorem says

`0 >= 6 + 6 + 3 d_6 - 3 q_6`.

The only arithmetic possibilities are `(d_6,q_6)=(1,5),(1,6),(2,6)`. The exact deficiency-one singleton-capacity bound is three. The first case needs at least four singleton replacements and the second needs six, so both are impossible. Hence every one-row deletion has

`d_6=2, q_6=6`.

Because the total incomplete cost is six, the original seven-row state has exactly six singleton incomplete blocks and no pair block. All six singleton blocks are virtual-canonical complete in every one-row deletion.

Now delete any two no-root rows. Defect transport gives a five-row `D=2,k=3,p=6` shadow. Row-nullity monotonicity gives `d_5<=2`, while the repaired five-row `k=3` theorem gives `d_5>=1` and

`2 >= 5 + 6 + 3 d_5 - 3 q_5`.

If `d_5=1`, then `q_5>=4`. But all six actual incomplete blocks are singletons, while the exact five-row deficiency-one singleton-capacity maximum is three. Therefore `d_5=2`, and then `q_5>=5`.

Thus every hypothetical counterexample produces a six-row rank-two state satisfying all of the following:

1. every coordinate projection still has rank two;
2. its virtual canonical deficiency is `d_6=2`;
3. at least six virtual-canonical complete blocks admit compatible singleton states;
4. every one-coordinate projection has five-row virtual deficiency `d_5=2`;
5. in each such projection at least five of the same six singleton blocks remain virtual-canonical complete.

The branch is therefore reduced to a finite synchronized six-row audit.

